nd ignominy.", "tags": ["uncountable", "usually"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "guides who are the shame of religion", "reference": "1692–1717, Robert South, Twelve Sermons Preached upon Several Occasions, volume (please specify |volume=I to VI), London:"}, {"text": "Rimmer ducked his body low into his chair, so just his head remained above the table top, and peered past the backs of the examinees in front of him, waiting for the adjudicator to make his move. Waiting for him to leap forward and rip off his flimsy flightsuit, exposing his shame: his illustrated body, Rimmer's cheating frame.", "reference": "1989, Grant Naylor, Red Dwarf: Infinity Welcomes Careful Drivers:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "ba", "name": "Bashkir"}, "word": "оят"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "häpeä"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "nolous"}, {"language": {"code": "vi", "name": "Vietnamese"}, "word": "mối nhục"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "That which is shameful and private, especially private parts.", "tags": ["uncountable", "usually"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "And he took fig-leaves and sewed (them) together, and made an apron for himself, and covered his shame.", "reference": "1902, R. H. Charles, transl., The book of Jubilees, or The little Genesis, London: A. and C. Black, 3:22, page 26:"}, {"text": "She turns to lift her robe, and lays it across her as though she were revealing her shame, as though she were naked.", "reference": "1991, Martha Graham, Blood Memory, Washington Square Press:"}, {"text": "She didn't even have her handbag, because Zelda had thoughtfully left it in the kitchen along with her clothes. And nobody had even offered her so much as a T-shirt to cover her shame.", "reference": "2010, Jill Mansell, Millie's Fling, →ISBN:"}, {"text": "The trouble started early this morning when Pop was shoving his shirt and vest into his pants so he could c